# Arc summary — spec 0038 "Additional UI Tweaks"

*This is the narrative companion to the stats tool, **not a substitute for it.**
It records the human-readable story of the run — the decisions, surprises, and
dead-ends — that the numbers do not capture. Read it alongside `.stat-md` (the
numbers) and `.gap-analysis-md` (what shipped vs. what was asked); none is complete
without the others.*

> Note on provenance: the launching session started this arc **detached** (its own
> console, per the spec-0035 halt postmortem) and did not watch it live, so this
> summary is reconstructed after the fact from `.manifest-arc-runner.log`, the
> `.state.json`, and the resolver/judge artifacts rather than from live worker
> memory. The account below is faithful to those records.

## The short version

A clean arc. All 47 slices landed green and committed; the run ended `COMPLETE`
with **zero real escalations** — the arc never terminated to ask the operator —
no hangup, no operator intervention, and no manual overrides. If you only read one
line: nothing derailed, and the two-tier safety net (retry budget, then resolver)
did its job exactly once, deliberately.

## Time spent

End to end — from the spec-writer's first move to the last arc commit — the work
spanned about **32.1 hours** of calendar time (2026-07-10 17:50 UTC → 2026-07-12
01:56 UTC). Of that, roughly **1.8 hours** was spec authoring, a couple of hours
was the idle gap between the spec finishing and the arc being launched, and the
arc-runner phase itself was about **27.7 hours**. Within the arc, the honest split
is about **24.1 hours of active model compute** (the summed worker, judge, and
resolver run-times) and only about **3.6 hours of arc overhead** — gate runs,
commits, and the waits between slices. (The stats tool's `idle_gap_seconds` looks
much larger, ~25.8 h, but that metric counts a running worker's own execution as
"idle" because the runner logs nothing while a worker is busy; the real wait is the
~3.6 h overhead figure.)

## What happened on the way

The work was the usual UI-tweak mix — mostly `implement_worker` slices, plus a
handful of `add_component`, `add_contract`, and `implement_contract` slices, and a
final `wire_ui_worker` composition slice (047) that stitched the pieces together.
Every slice cleared all five gates (build · unit-tests · constructor-unit-tests ·
ui-smoke · ui-tests) and passed a code-judge review before commit. Final gate
counts at completion: 119 solver unit tests, 674 constructor unit tests, 180
ui-smoke tests, 462 ui-tests.

Three gate failures occurred across the whole arc, and all three self-recovered
inside the per-slice budget:

- **Slice 002** — the first attempt *regressed the constructor test count*
  (425 < baseline 447): the `count_at_least` gate caught it before it could commit
  a silent test deletion. The retry restored the count and passed.
- **Slice 020** — a plain build-gate failure (compile error) on attempt 1; the
  retry fixed it.
- **Slice 032** — the one genuinely interesting moment of the run (below).

## The one surprise worth knowing: slice 032 and the resolver

Slice 032 is the only slice that exhausted its 2/2 failure budget and reached the
resolver. It matters because the two failures were *not* the usual worker
thrashing:

1. **Attempt 1** short-circuited on the build gate with two round-one compile
   errors — a missing `open Berreman.Constants` for the `<nm>` unit-of-measure, and
   a `defaultState` open-collision.
2. **Attempt 2** fixed both surgically and went *all five gates green* — but the
   code-judge then routed it back. Its finding was precise and correct: the slice
   narrative mandates per-series show/hide toggles for the preview chart, and that
   feature (`ToggleSeriesVisibility` handler + `applyHidden`, keyed by
   `UiIds.seriesToggle`) shipped with **zero test coverage**. A wrong series index
   or tab code would have passed every gate silently.

Hitting 2/2 tripped the failures cap and invoked the resolver. The resolver's
call is the thing a future reader should note: it explicitly diagnosed this as
**not an oscillation cap** — the two failures were distinct and *progressive*
(a converging slice), not a worker oscillating between fixes. It verified the
judge's gap against the tree, issued a high-confidence `issue-hint` pointing at
the exact missing test (add/remove round-trip on the handler + prove `applyHidden`
lowers the named curve), bumped the route-back cap +1, and sent it back. Attempt 3
added the focused test and closed the slice green. This resolver rescue is *not* an
escalation — it kept the arc alive rather than stopping it for the operator — which
is exactly why the escalation count stays at zero: the safety net caught a real
coverage gap and distinguished "legitimately needs one more pass" from "stuck."

## Dead-ends and non-events

There were none of the dramatic kind — no approach was tried and abandoned, no
gate failed in a way that revealed a wrong assumption in the spec. The only boot
note was an expected `WARN`: the manifest omits several soft-required header keys
(retry/resolver budgets), so the loader filled process defaults. That is normal
for this manifest shape and did not affect the run.

The detached-launch decision (from the earlier spec-0035 mid-arc kill) is
vindicated here: unlike that run, this one was never touched by the harness and
ran uninterrupted from slice 001 to 047.
